{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q007_chey_like_function_divergence","answer":"Supported relationship: in SCOPe all three domains are classified in the same superfamily, c.23.1 'CheY-like', within fold c.23 'Flavodoxin-like' (three-layer a/b/a sandwich; five-stranded parallel sheet, order 21345). They occupy separate families: c.23.1.1 CheY-related (CheY, e.g. E. coli CheY 3chy; Spo0F; receiver domains of NarL, FixJ, PhoB, Spo0A), c.23.1.2 Ornithine decarboxylase N-terminal 'wing' domain (eukaryotic ODC, e.g. mouse 7odc, human 1d7k), and c.23.1.3 KaiA/RbsU domain (Synechococcus KaiA N-terminal domain, 1m2e/1m2f, 1r8j). Superfamily placement is SCOPe's assertion of probable common evolutionary origin inferred from shared structural and functional features, not from detectable sequence similarity; sequence-level similarity (~>30% identity or clear functional conservation) is expected only within a family. The fold level (c.23) is shared with many non-homologous superfamilies (flavoproteins, class I glutamine amidotransferase-like, etc.), so fold similarity alone supports no evolutionary claim.

Claims that are not supported / must not transfer: (1) Receiver-domain signaling belongs to c.23.1.1 only. The conserved phosphoacceptor Asp (Asp57 in CheY) with its Mg2+-binding acidic pocket (Asp12/Asp13), the Thr87/Lys109 switch residues, phosphotransfer from a sensor histidine kinase, and output coupling (FliM binding for CheY; DNA-binding output domains, which are separate SCOPe domains, for NarL/FixJ/PhoB) cannot be assigned to the ODC wing or the KaiA N-terminal domain. (2) The KaiA N-terminal domain is a pseudo-receiver: it lacks the phosphorylatable Asp and other conserved receiver residues, so it is not a kinase substrate; its circadian role is to receive input and modulate the separate all-alpha C-terminal domain that binds KaiC and stimulates KaiC autophosphorylation. Neither KaiC binding nor clock-modulating activity can be inferred from the c.23.1 fold. (3) The ODC N-terminal 'wing' domain is non-catalytic; it contributes to dimerization/antizyme interactions. PLP-dependent decarboxylation occurs at the dimer interface formed by the alpha/beta PLP-binding barrel (c.1.6, Alanine racemase-like N-terminal domain) and the beta-sheet domain (b.49.2, Alanine racemase C-terminal domain-like), so no decarboxylase chemistry can be attributed to any CheY-like domain. (4) Conversely, no catalytic or pseudo-receiver function from ODC or KaiA transfers to response regulators. Even within c.23.1.1, downstream function varies, so 'response regulator' implies receiver chemistry only, not a specific output.

Limits on templates and representatives: a CheY template can model the shared alpha/beta core backbone, but the active-site loops, the alpha4-beta5-alpha5 signaling surface, and the additional C-terminal strand noted by SCOP for the ODC wing differ; identity-filtered ASTRAL representative sets (e.g. 40% identity) will place the three families in different representatives, so a c.23.1.1 representative is a low-confidence template for c.23.1.2 or c.23.1.3. Any function transfer requires external evidence: a conserved acceptor Asp plus in vitro phosphorylation/kinase assays for receiver activity, enzyme kinetics with PLP for decarboxylation, and KaiC-binding or circadian phenotype data for KaiA.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge","questions_blind.jsonl (question record supplied in prompt)"]}]}
